      "year": 2008,
      "abstract": "What happens to our notion of political obligation when right is divorced from moral considerations? When one says that an individual\u2019s claim to her property is a right that ought not to be abridged, on what kind of nonmoral principle can one rely? According to Matthew Sean Pines, Fichte believes that he can ground such a normative prescription on a theory of natural right, deriving a set of strict political principles from a necessary metaphysical conception of a rational being. It is the main task of this paper to work through this difficult deduction, assess its validity, and discuss the general implications of its result. What is said here concerning Fichte\u2019s specific proposal for an objective theory of natural\u2014as opposed to moral\u2014right bears broader significance for the fundamental problem of the nature and source of obligation in the political world.",
