    {
      "paperId": "42f7ad13c31a78cf4c36c4a6538353088b7b2268",
      "title": "Kant and Hegel: natural law or philosophy of right?",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "A. Krouglov",
          "authorId": "2387733967"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "In modern Russian legal scholarship, a widespread view holds that there are two dis\u00adtinct traditions \u2013 on the one hand, natural law, and on the other, the philosophy of right. The key representatives of the latter are I. Kant and G.W.F. Hegel. A study of philosophi\u00adcal and legal treatises from the last decade of the 18th century to the first quarter of the 19th century reveals that Kant\u2019s \u201cMetaphysics of Morals\u201d (\u201cDoctrine of Right\u201d) and Hegel\u2019s \u201cPhilosophy of Right\u201d represent only the pinnacle of a broader wave of philo\u00adsophical-legal literature devoted to natural law (G. Hufeland, L.J.F. H\u00f6pfner, K.L. Rein\u00adhold, J.Chr.G. Schaumann, K.H. Heydenreich, Th. Schmalz, K.L. P\u00f6rschke, L.H. Jakob, G.S.A. Mellin, J.G. Fichte, D.W.J. Schelling, S. Maimon, P.J.A. Feuerbach, etc.). Both works were conceived within this context, and their authors \u2013 as well as their contempo\u00adraries \u2013 unambiguously perceived them as contributions to natural law theory. Although Kant did not yet employ the term \u201cphilosophy of right\u201d, Hegel\u2019s treatment of natural law and philosophy of right as synonyms demonstrates that a rigid opposition between the two is an artificial construct. The neglect of the German natural law tradition in Rus\u00adsian academia obscures both the conventional elements and the genuine innovations in the works of Kant and Hegel.",
